{"h":3396,"w":2400,"lines":[{"h":0.014671783986445419,"c":1,"x":0.08432997629324783,"y":0.033361782242555371,"w":0.061792723337809261,"t":"Test 3"},{"c":1,"w":0.016460905075073237,"y":0.31104651137616757,"t":"F","h":0.014534883982160851,"x":0.084362139952175313},{"c":1,"y":0.079669692590107588,"h":0.019670747053777693,"x":0.1275218235773094,"t":"In their active state, phytochrome molecules bind themselves to DNA to restrict","w":0.74703689575195309},{"h":0.018967252176417371,"c":1,"w":0.71810699462890626,"x":0.12962963015055473,"t":"plant growth. During the day, sunlight activates the molecules, slowing down","y":0.10029069806334845},{"y":0.11918604690033496,"c":1,"w":0.73045267740885411,"h":0.018917604947399025,"x":0.12962962828181901,"t":"growth. If a plant finds itself in shade, phytochromes are quickly inactivated -"},{"x":0.12962962009488327,"h":0.017441860778592888,"y":0.13808139518846518,"c":1,"t":"enabling it to grow faster to find sunlight again. This is how plants compete to","w":0.72839508056640634},{"w":0.78806584676106772,"c":1,"h":0.017469252798666846,"t":"escape each other's shade. 'Light-driven changes to phytochrome activity occur very","x":0.12962962796576921,"y":0.15694935200566018},{"h":0.022616693072100014,"t":"fast, in less than a second,' says Wigge.","x":0.12948766911575935,"c":1,"y":0.17381020083921728,"w":0.36855110168457028},{"c":1,"h":0.020090643452530865,"t":"At night, however, it's a different story. Instead of a rapid deactivation following","x":0.12956838889424935,"y":0.20419411591989567,"w":0.74498428344726564},{"c":1,"y":0.22230104412226859,"t":"sundown, the molecules gradually change from their active to inactive state. This is","x":0.12952523810591893,"w":0.77800059000651034,"h":0.022257490349040676},{"w":0.75720163981119792,"h":0.018989507947007378,"x":0.12962963267504221,"c":1,"y":0.24263839863506231,"t":"called 'dark reversion'. 'Just as mercury rises in a thermometer, the rate at which"},{"t":"phytochromes revert to their inactive state during the night is a direct measure of","h":0.019140164899882239,"x":0.12962963287995971,"y":0.26148409911485149,"w":0.75720163981119781,"c":1},{"h":0.020419029801978628,"c":1,"x":0.12962963453307186,"y":0.28052325546151846,"t":"temperature, says Wigge.","w":0.24485595703125002},{"x":0.12962962636260186,"t":"ẠThe lower the temperature, the slower the rate at which phytochromes revert to","y":0.31095406401895131,"h":0.018987796051061268,"w":0.74279836018880219,"c":1},{"t":"inactivity, so the molecules spend more time in their active, growth-suppressing","w":0.74496785481770833,"x":0.12957002201299758,"h":0.020133892414286203,"y":0.32842101209425656,"c":1},{"x":0.12962961979892051,"w":0.78600824991861973,"h":0.017476100382451065,"y":0.34883720914286198,"c":1,"t":"state. This is why plants are slower to grow in winter. Warm temperatures accelerate"},{"c":1,"x":0.13158443577936491,"w":0.73475011189778638,"t":"dark reversion, so that phytochromes rapidly reach an inactive state and detach","y":0.36544445820009341,"h":0.02169763185391016},{"y":0.38625473203651772,"w":0.79025680541992194,"h":0.019834008458365471,"c":1,"x":0.1295595059607704,"t":"themselves from the plant's DNA - allowing genes to be expressed and plant growth"},{"y":0.40439892911229025,"c":1,"w":0.75734049479166665,"t":"to resume.' Wigge believes phytochrome thermo-sensing evolved at a later stage,","h":0.021496003311570533,"x":0.12957239912967716},{"h":0.017518900589330699,"t":"and co-opted the biological network already used for light-based growth during the","c":1,"x":0.12962963803164976,"y":0.4257950530527197,"w":0.77777776082356775},{"t":"downtime of night.","h":0.016195524146054296,"x":0.13168724260642967,"c":1,"y":0.44611307445592852,"w":0.18106995900472006},{"w":0.77983540852864586,"h":0.018958692696687152,"t":"Some plants mainly use day length as an indicator of the season. Other species, such","x":0.12962962162730945,"y":0.47232037665468418,"c":1},{"h":0.019850163633888029,"x":0.13161121598120701,"w":0.73883356730143235,"c":1,"y":0.49211400928325566,"t":"as daffodils, have considerable temperature sensitivity, and can flower months in"},{"w":0.79218109130859382,"y":0.51148409911887049,"h":0.019140164899882239,"c":1,"t":"advance during a warm winter. In fact, the discovery of the dual role of phytochromes","x":0.13168723205731284},{"t":"provides the science behind a well-known rhyme long used to predict the coming","y":0.52892554975096129,"x":0.13153587494270766,"w":0.74718007405598952,"h":0.022334669447899969,"c":1},{"h":0.022223311964558512,"x":0.13159103589857693,"c":1,"w":0.7162276713053386,"y":0.548899181624665,"t":"season: oak before ash we'll have a splash, ash before oak we're in for a soak."},{"w":0.67283950805664061,"c":1,"x":0.13374486109513631,"h":0.018979236571331048,"t":"Wigge explains: 'Oak trees rely much more on temperature, likely using","y":0.57848837229591554},{"h":0.017476100382450954,"y":0.59883720914102123,"x":0.13168724591001307,"c":1,"t":"phytochromes as thermometers to dictate development, whereas ash trees rely on","w":0.75720163981119781},{"t":"measuring day length to determine their seasonal timing. A warmer spring, and","y":0.61665978458042447,"w":0.74090591430664055,"h":0.02048138145563605,"x":0.13161124793106077,"c":1},{"y":0.63584931060455097,"w":0.76146331787109367,"h":0.020164110073633279,"t":"consequently a higher likeliness of a hot summer, will result in oak leafing before","c":1,"x":0.1316213720716948},{"y":0.65547703164224302,"h":0.018941572613935143,"x":0.13168723392422549,"w":0.77983540852864586,"c":1,"t":"ash. A cold spring will see the opposite. As the British know only too well, a colder"},{"x":0.13155017551651013,"w":0.39328501383463543,"h":0.019691475148195359,"y":0.67436896471056251,"t":"summer is likely to be a rain-soaked one.?'","c":1},{"t":"The new findings are the culmination of twelve years of research involving scientists","x":0.13367562140719977,"w":0.79025370279947915,"h":0.019792610680677836,"c":1,"y":0.7029984600346062},{"x":0.13374485436178504,"h":0.018939860717989143,"t":"from Germany, Argentina and the US, as well as the Cambridge team. The work was","y":0.72379269702511362,"w":0.78806584676106772,"c":1},{"t":"done in a model system, using a mustard plant called Arabidopsis, but Wigge says","w":0.76559417724609369,"h":0.021147118019130939,"y":0.74117313072009672,"x":0.13366118780977407,"c":1},{"h":0.020119197517457943,"x":0.13161715314318617,"w":0.79437733968098967,"y":0.76086845050591168,"c":1,"t":"the phytochrome genes necessary for temperature sensing are found in crop plants as"},{"w":0.78189300537109374,"h":0.018946708301773363,"c":1,"x":0.1337448552395826,"t":"well. 'Recent advances in plant genetics now mean that scientists are able to rapidly","y":0.78052325620302865},{"y":0.79787639952908573,"c":1,"w":0.75128026326497399,"h":0.023156337940229532,"t":"identify the genes controlling these processes in crop plants, and even alter their","x":0.13156718348797425},{"w":0.75514404296875004,"h":0.020348837575025036,"c":1,"y":0.81831395330206169,"x":0.13374485084840027,"t":"activity using precise molecular \"scalpels\", adds Wigge. 'Cambridge is uniquely"},{"x":0.13366997124635258,"w":0.74294006347656261,"h":0.019298989303822456,"t":"well-positioned to do this kind of research as we have outstanding collaborators","y":0.83841522355561848,"c":1},{"h":0.017501780506578912,"x":0.13374484713983367,"y":0.85895170801827025,"w":0.77983540852864586,"c":1,"t":"nearby who work on more applied aspects of plant biology, and can help us transter"},{"w":0.32401138305664062,"c":1,"t":"this new knowledge into the field.?","h":0.025104228402756723,"x":0.13334004129977273,"y":0.87440999920253826},{"y":0.90843023238608001,"w":0.026748971939086902,"t":"70","x":0.086419752619276841,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1},{"c":1,"w":0.25425661722819015,"t":"IELTSXpress.com","y":0.9556315279170291,"h":0.027004518273019973,"x":0.71953327191129191}]}